<p>
  Part 2 ends where the code stops being the problem. Every case in it was a fix you could point at in a
  diff: an isolation level, a missing index, a transaction boundary, a rendering engine. What follows are
  the five battles that came after — the ones fought in the space <em>between</em> the jobs rather than
  inside them, where the code is innocent and the topology is the product.
</p>
<p>
  The cases stay numbered continuously with Part 2, because they happened in the same two years and often
  in the same week. Every worker count below is the production and pre-production figure unless stated
  otherwise: the same file collapses to one replica per queue in development and sandbox, so "22 queues"
  does not mean 122 processes everywhere. As before, each one tells you <strong style="color:var(--incident)">what was actually
  going wrong</strong>, <strong style="color:var(--muted)">what we tried</strong> — including the attempts
  that had to be reverted — and <strong style="color:var(--accent)">what the numbers said</strong>
  afterwards.
</p>


<section class="era" id="case-07">
  <div class="era-head"><h2>Case 07 · The Ghost Latency Between Jobs</h2></div>
  <p class="case-hook"><em>Application telemetry told us a job took 0.5 seconds. End-to-end telemetry told us it took 7.5 seconds. We were optimizing service time, completely blind to the silent killer: queue wait time.</em></p>
  <p class="impact-pill"><span class="lab">Impact</span><span class="val">wait ~118s → ≈0 · 5,000 daily</span></p>
  <div class="incident">
    <span class="tag">The problem</span>
    <p>By mid-2026, 5,000-order runs were routine — and the daily test showed the Borrower Ownership step
    taking <strong>around two minutes</strong> for 4,000 of the 5,000 orders. The decisive clue came from an
    exercise anyone can copy, and we ran it first on the purchase step next door: add up the actual CPU time
    of every job in the step and compare it with the wall clock. The jobs came to
    <span class="k-cost">521 milliseconds, inside a step that observably took seven and a half
    seconds</span>. The same arithmetic on Borrower Ownership told the same story on a crueller scale —
    about a second of work inside two minutes of elapsed time. <span class="k-rule">The code was innocent in both cases; the
    difference was time spent standing in line.</span><a class="refmark" href="#ref-queue-wait">queue wait ↓</a> And queue wait has a cruel personality: <span class="k-cost">invisible when the
    system is idle (~0s), noticeable under load (~7s at 91 orders a minute), catastrophic at peak
    (~118 seconds)</span>.</p>
  </div>

    <GhostLatencyWidget />





  <div class="attempt">
    <span class="tag">What we tried — and the bottleneck that moved</span>
    <p>The fix was to stop making unrelated work share one line.<a class="refmark" href="#ref-queues">queues ↓</a> Ownership transfers and auto-sales had been
    competing for the same eight workers; each got its own dedicated queue on its own server, and the
    two-minute wait evaporated.</p>
    <p>Then the fix backfired — upstream. With that backpressure gone, the <em>purchase</em> step nearly
    tripled, from 3.6 to 9.9 seconds. All the throughput we had just freed slammed into a 700,000-row table
    that had <strong>no index on the one column every duplicate-check searched</strong> — a full table scan
    that had been hiding behind the queue the entire time. One index later, the job was back under a
    second.</p>
    <p>The rest was housekeeping that only matters at this scale: fifteen log lines per order deleted
    (about 100&nbsp;MB of logs a day at 5,000 orders), unused indexes dropped to cut write cost, and
    explicit connection budgets after the full worker fleet exhausted the database's connections.</p>
<div class="codebox">
  <span class="cap">Migration — the index the upstream lookup had been missing</span>
  <pre><code>/**
 * local_market_orders has ~700k rows and no index on external_order_no, so
 * every lookup by reference (the duplicate check in CreateLocalMarketOrderAction
 * and LocalMarketTrait::getLocalMarketOrderByReference used by transfer/sell/
 * cancel) runs a full table scan. Under the perf-test burst these concurrent
 * scans thrash the buffer pool and inflate the initiate-order step. A plain
 * index turns them into single-row lookups (LYNKMRBHA-6509).
 */
public function up(): void
&#123;
    Schema::table('local_market_orders', function (Blueprint $table) &#123;
        $table-&gt;index('external_order_no', 'local_market_orders_external_order_no_index');
    &#125;);
&#125;</code></pre>
</div>

  </div>

