  <div class="attempt">
    <span class="tag">How we manage it</span>
    <p><strong>The fleet is a file, not folklore.</strong><a class="refmark" href="#ref-horizon">horizon ↓</a> Every queue's worker count, per machine, is
    declared in the deployment script — so the entire topology arrives in a pull request instead of being
    discovered by SSH-ing into a box, and when a number changes the reason is in the commit. The catch is
    that a queue lives in <em>four</em> places: the Compose profile list, the steady-state scale block, the
    rolling-deploy scale block, and the service list the deploy iterates over. Miss one and the queue is
    deployed but not scaled, or scaled but never restarted onto the new image.</p>
    <p><strong>"Groups" are machines.</strong> The three Compose profiles map one-to-one onto physical
    servers in pre-production and production; the deploy runs all three in parallel, one per node. In
    development and sandbox the same file collapses onto a single box at one replica per queue. One
    description, three shapes — which is the only reason a fleet this size stays reviewable.</p>
    <p><strong>We add where the measurement points, not where the anxiety is.</strong> Every row in the
    table above began as a timing measurement showing a queue whose jobs were demonstrably waiting rather
    than working. None of them began as "this feels slow."</p>
    <p><strong>And we remove just as deliberately.</strong> Dead workers get deleted, misleading names get
    corrected, and a queue is drained to zero before it is renamed — because renaming a queue while jobs are
    still sitting in it is how you lose transactions. One queue is pinned at a <em>single worker</em>
    forever: the step where parallelism cannot be made safe. In a case study full of scaling-up decisions,
    the most important number in the fleet is a one.</p>
  </div>
<div class="codebox">
  <span class="cap">The deploy script — worker scale flags, per environment</span>
  <pre><code>if [[ "$DEPLOY_ENV" == "sandbox" || "$DEPLOY_ENV" == "dev" ]]; then
    # Sandbox/Dev: 1 replica per worker type (all groups on 1 server),
    # except default-worker=4 as the default queue carries the two
    # sequential client-wakala jobs (ask + proceed) and backs up under load.
    # Keeps total DB connections ~28 instead of ~86 at full production scale
    scale_flags="--scale local-market-eligible-quantities-worker=1"
    # ...
else
    # Production/preprod: full scale per group
    if [[ "$PROFILES" == *"group1"* ]]; then
        scale_flags="$scale_flags --scale local-market-order-inventories-units-creation=8"
        scale_flags="$scale_flags --scale local-market-order-initiation=1"
        scale_flags="$scale_flags --scale trader-order-initiation=10"
        # ...
    fi

    if [[ "$PROFILES" == *"group2"* ]]; then
        scale_flags="$scale_flags --scale local-market-transfer-ownership-worker=4"
        scale_flags="$scale_flags --scale local-market-sell-to-open-market-worker=4"
        # ...
    fi
fi</code></pre>
</div>

  <div class="incident">
    <span class="tag">Where a fleet bites back</span>
    <p><strong>A duplicate flag silently halves a queue.</strong><a class="refmark" href="#ref-replicas">replicas ↓</a> Compose keeps only the last value when the
    same service is scaled twice, so merging two profile groups onto one host quietly drops one queue from
    8 workers to 4 and another from 5 to 2. No error, no warning — just a slower system and a load-test
    result that means nothing.</p>
    <p><strong>One undefined YAML anchor blocks every deploy.</strong> A Compose file referenced a
    <code>*graceful-stop</code> anchor that had never been defined on that branch. <code>docker compose
    config</code> failed — and since every deploy command parses the file first, <em>all</em> of them broke
    at once.</p>
    <p><strong>Deleting a service does not stop its containers.</strong> Removing a worker from the file
    leaves the running containers behind unless the deploy is explicitly told to remove orphans — and even
    then, containers whose profile is no longer active survive the sweep. Five such orphans came back after
    a routine reboot, restarted by their own restart policy, quietly serving a three-week-old image.</p>
    <p><span class="k-rule">The real ceiling is connections, not CPU.</span><a class="refmark" href="#ref-connections">connections ↓</a> In June 2026 a smaller environment was
    running all three groups at production replicas on one server: <strong>86 worker containers against
    MySQL's default connection limit of 151</strong>, with an unbounded pool of web processes competing for what
    was left. The fix was a budget rather than a bigger database — web processes capped at 10 and recycled
    every 500 requests, small environments pinned to one replica per queue, which brought the whole
    environment to about 28 connections instead of 86.</p>
    <p><strong>Retiring a machine can retire the scheduler.</strong> The scheduler's profile was attached to
    the third server group, so decommissioning that group would have stopped every cron job on the platform
    without stopping anything anyone was watching. Caught while planning the consolidation; the trigger
    moved with it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="metric">
    <span class="tag">The result</span>
    The fleet grew from <span class="k-win">3 worker containers in January 2025 to 122 processes across 3
    machines</span> — the production and pre-production figure — and unlike the platform of 2024, every
    one of those numbers has a commit explaining it. The proof that the discipline is real is that it now runs in reverse: in August 2026 pre-production
    went from three machines to two with <strong>total queue concurrency unchanged</strong>, and the hardest
    part was proving that the second number had not quietly moved. The goal was never
    "more workers." It was <em>the right workers, in the right places, for reasons written down.</em>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="era" id="case-10">
  <div class="era-head"><h2>Case 10 · The Phantom Deployments</h2></div>
  <p class="case-hook"><em>A perfectly successful deployment left the background workers silently executing the old application state. It was a humbling reminder that persistent memory requires persistent orchestration.</em></p>
  <p class="impact-pill"><span class="lab">Impact</span><span class="val">stale image → every worker on the build</span></p>
  <div class="incident">
    <span class="tag">The problem</span>
    <p>Jobs were finishing and their follow-up jobs were not starting. The chain simply stopped — no
    exception, no failed job, nothing in the logs to attach blame to. The follow-up jobs existed; they were
    sitting in a database table that nothing was reading.</p>
    <p>The workers were running an image from an earlier deploy, and baked into that image was an old
    environment file naming the <em>database</em> queue driver — from before the platform moved to Redis.
    The application dispatched to Redis. The workers, still on the old build, wrote and read the database
    table. Every follow-up job in every chain landed in a queue nobody was watching.</p>
    <p><span class="k-cost">Deploys had been reporting success the whole time.</span><a class="refmark" href="#ref-deploy">deploys ↓</a> The web container picked up new code on every
    release; the workers had not picked up any.</p>
  </div>

  <PhantomDeployDiagram />
  <div class="attempt">
    <span class="tag">What we tried</span>
    <p>Two separate things had to be true before a worker could ever receive a build. The first was that
    Compose cannot detect that the contents behind a fixed tag have changed — every service referenced
    <code>:latest</code>, so <code>docker compose up</code> saw no reason to replace a container that was
    already running. Making the build's commit hash the image tag turns "new code" into something the tool
    can actually see.</p>
    <p>That alone did not fix it. The rolling deploy scaled workers up with <code>--no-recreate</code>,
    which suppresses image swaps outright, and only the web container had an explicit force-recreate — so
    the scale up-and-down never replaced a single worker. Workers got their own force-recreate, scoped to
    the worker profiles and with <code>--no-deps</code>, because an earlier unscoped attempt had
    force-recreated the databases along with everything else and had to be reverted.</p>
    <p>The same audit turned up a tax nobody had costed. Every worker was taking its full ten-second stop
    grace and then being killed outright on each deploy — the bulk of a roughly 140-second scale-down. The
    <code>pcntl</code> extension had never been installed, so the queue worker could not trap stop signals
    at all, and the stop signal inherited from the base image was not one it would have handled anyway.
    Installing the extension and sending the signal the worker actually listens for dropped idle shutdown
    from about ten seconds to about one.</p>
<div class="codebox">
  <span class="cap">The deploy script — the rolling worker restart</span>
  <pre><code># Scale up: new containers alongside old ones
docker compose -p lynk-backend $PROFILE_FLAGS up -d --no-recreate $SCALE_UP_FLAGS

# ...

# Scale down to target
docker compose -p lynk-backend $PROFILE_FLAGS up -d $SCALE_FLAGS

# Force-recreate all PHP services to ensure no old image digest survives.
# We keep this explicit to avoid recreating infrastructure services
# such as redis/mysql/mailhog when their profile is enabled.
PHP_SERVICES=$(get_php_service_names)
PHP_SERVICES=$(filter_existing_services "$PHP_SERVICES")
if [ -n "$PHP_SERVICES" ]; then
    docker compose -p lynk-backend $PROFILE_FLAGS up -d --force-recreate --no-deps \
        $SCALE_FLAGS $PHP_SERVICES
fi</code></pre>
</div>

  </div>
  <div class="metric">
    <span class="tag">The result</span>
    Every deploy now replaces every worker with the build it claims to have deployed, and a fleet of 122
    containers stops in roughly the time one used to. The lesson generalizes past this stack: <strong>a
    deploy that only verifies the web tier is not a deploy</strong>. On a platform like this one, the web
    tier is a thin edge in front of the queues — the workers are where the code actually runs.
  </div>
</section>



<section class="refs" id="further-reading">
  <h2>Further reading</h2>
  <p>This series names more than it can teach — a contract type here, an isolation level there, a queueing effect somewhere else. Where a term is carrying real weight in the text above, this is the primary source to go and read properly, rather than a summary of a summary. Every link was checked when this was published.</p>
  <ul>
    <li id="ref-queues"><strong>Workers, connections and queues</strong> (Cases <a class="caseref" href="#case-07">07</a>, <a class="caseref" href="#case-09">09</a>) — the vocabulary this whole part
      is built on, including why one worker process serves one queue list at a time:
      <a href="https://laravel.com/docs/12.x/queues">Laravel queues</a>.</li>
    <li id="ref-unique"><strong>Uniqueness is a lock, not a guarantee</strong> (Case <a class="caseref" href="#case-08">08</a>) — what
      <code>ShouldBeUnique</code> does and, importantly, what it does not promise if the lock expires:
      <a href="https://laravel.com/docs/12.x/queues#unique-jobs">unique jobs</a>. The self-expiring locks used elsewhere in the flow
      are <a href="https://laravel.com/docs/12.x/cache#atomic-locks">atomic locks</a>.</li>
    <li id="ref-retries"><strong>Retries, backoff, and why jitter matters</strong> (Case <a class="caseref" href="#case-08">08</a>) —
      <a href="https://laravel.com/docs/12.x/queues#dealing-with-failed-jobs">Laravel's failed-job handling</a> for the mechanics, and
      the <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/">AWS
      Builders' Library on timeouts, retries and backoff with jitter</a> for why synchronised retries
      make an overloaded system worse.</li>
    <li id="ref-deploy"><strong>Deploying without killing in-flight jobs</strong> (Case <a class="caseref" href="#case-10">10</a>) — why a worker keeps running
      old code until it is told not to: <a href="https://laravel.com/docs/12.x/queues#queue-workers-and-deployment">queue workers and
      deployment</a>.</li>
    <li id="ref-horizon"><strong>The dashboard we did not use.</strong> If you are sizing a Redis-backed fleet today, read
      <a href="https://laravel.com/docs/12.x/horizon">Horizon</a> first — its balancing strategies solve much of Case <a class="caseref" href="#case-09">09</a> for you.</li>
    <li id="ref-brpop"><strong>What a queue worker is actually doing while it waits</strong> — the blocking pop underneath
      a Redis queue: <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/commands/brpop/">Redis BRPOP</a>.</li>
    <li id="ref-queue-wait"><strong>Why 0.52 seconds of work sat inside a 7.5-second step</strong> (Case <a class="caseref" href="#case-07">07</a>) — the difference
      between service time and queue wait, and how to find saturation:
      <a href="https://www.brendangregg.com/usemethod.html">Brendan Gregg's USE method</a>. For why
      adding workers eventually stops helping — and then hurts — see Neil Gunther's
      <a href="http://www.perfdynamics.com/Manifesto/USLscalability.html">Universal Scalability
      Law</a>.</li>
    <li id="ref-connections"><strong>Sizing processes against a connection ceiling</strong> — the knob that was
      unbounded, and the ceiling it ran into:
      <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/install.fpm.configuration.php">PHP-FPM process manager
      configuration</a> and MySQL's
      <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/server-system-variables.html#sysvar_max_connections">max_connections</a>.</li>
    <li id="ref-replicas"><strong>Declaring replica counts</strong> (Case <a class="caseref" href="#case-09">09</a>) — how many copies of a service run,
      whether that is declared in the compose file or passed as a scale flag at deploy time:
      <a href="https://docs.docker.com/reference/compose-file/deploy/">Compose deploy specification</a>.</li>
    <li id="ref-healthcheck"><strong>Health checks that touch the application</strong> — a probe that only proves a
      port is open will call a dead application healthy:
      <a href="https://microservices.io/patterns/observability/health-check-api.html">health check API
      pattern</a>, and <a href="https://12factor.net/disposability">12-factor disposability</a> for why a
      worker must survive being killed mid-job.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

